PPOORRTTAABBIILLIITTYY
       Over time _n_c_u_r_s_e_s ttppuutt has differed from that of System V in two impor‐
       tant respects, one now mostly historical.

       •   “ttppuutt  _c_a_p_-_c_o_d_e” writes to the standard output, which need not be a
           terminal device.  However, the operands  that  manipulate  terminal
           modes might not use the standard output.

           System V  ttppuutt's  iinniitt  and  rreesseett  operands use logic from 4.1cBSD
           ttsseett, manipulating terminal modes.  It checks  the  same  file  de‐
           scriptors  (and _/_d_e_v_/_t_t_y) for association with a terminal device as
           _n_c_u_r_s_e_s now does, and if none are, finally assumes a 1200 baud ter‐
           minal.  When updating terminal modes, it ignores errors.

           Until  _n_c_u_r_s_e_s 6.1 (see section “HISTORY” below), ttppuutt did not mod‐
           ify terminal modes.  It now employs a scheme similar  to  System V,
           using  functions  shared  with ttsseett (and ultimately based on 4.4BSD
           ttsseett).  If it is not able to open a terminal  (for  instance,  when
           run by _c_r_o_n(1)), ttppuutt exits with an error status.

       •   System V  ttppuutt  assumes  that the type of a _c_a_p_-_c_o_d_e operand is nu‐
           meric if all the characters of its value are  decimal  numbers;  if
           they are not, it treats _c_a_p_-_c_o_d_e as a string capability.

           Most implementations that provide support for _c_a_p_-_c_o_d_e operands use
           the ttppaarrmm(3X) function to expand its parameters.  That function ex‐
           pects a mixture of numeric and string parameters, requiring ttppuutt to
           know which type to use.

           _n_c_u_r_s_e_s ttppuutt uses a table to determine the parameter types for  the
           standard  _c_a_p_-_c_o_d_e  operands,  and  an internal function to analyze
           nonstandard _c_a_p_-_c_o_d_e operands.

           While more reliable than System V's utility, a portability  problem
           is  introduced  by this analysis.  An OpenBSD developer adapted the
           internal library function from _n_c_u_r_s_e_s to  port  NetBSD's  _t_e_r_m_c_a_p-
           based  ttppuutt to _t_e_r_m_i_n_f_o, and modified it to interpret multiple _c_a_p_-
           _c_o_d_e_s (and parameters) on the command line.  Portable  applications
           should not rely upon this feature; _n_c_u_r_s_e_s offers it to support ap‐
           plications written specifically for OpenBSD.

       This implementation, unlike others, accepts both _t_e_r_m_c_a_p  and  _t_e_r_m_i_n_f_o
       _c_a_p_-_c_o_d_e_s  if  _t_e_r_m_c_a_p  support is compiled in.  In that case, however,
       the predefined _t_e_r_m_c_a_p and _t_e_r_m_i_n_f_o codes have two ambiguities; _n_c_u_r_s_e_s
       assumes the _t_e_r_m_i_n_f_o code.

       •   The  _c_a_p_-_c_o_d_e  ddll means ddeelleettee__lliinnee to _t_e_r_m_c_a_p but ppaarrmm__ddeelleettee__lliinnee
           to _t_e_r_m_i_n_f_o.  _t_e_r_m_c_a_p uses the code DDLL for ppaarrmm__ddeelleettee__lliinnee.  _t_e_r_m_‐
           _i_n_f_o uses the code ddll11 for ddeelleettee__lliinnee.

       •   The  _c_a_p_-_c_o_d_e  eedd  means eexxiitt__ddeelleettee__mmooddee to _t_e_r_m_c_a_p but ccllrr__eeooss to
           _t_e_r_m_i_n_f_o.  _t_e_r_m_c_a_p uses the code ccdd for ccllrr__eeooss.  _t_e_r_m_i_n_f_o uses the
           code rrmmddcc for eexxiitt__ddeelleettee__mmooddee.

       The  lloonnggnnaammee  operand,  --SS option, and the parameter-substitution fea‐
       tures used in the ccuupp example below, were  not  supported  in  AT&T/USL
       _c_u_r_s_e_s before SVr4 (1989).  Later, 4.3BSD-Reno (1990) added support for
       lloonnggnnaammee, and in 1994, NetBSD added support for the parameter-substitu‐
       tion features.

       IEEE   Std   1003.1/The   Open   Group   Base  Specifications  Issue  7
       (POSIX.1-2008) documents only the cclleeaarr, iinniitt, and rreesseett  operands.   A
       few observations of interest arise from that selection.

       •   _n_c_u_r_s_e_s supports cclleeaarr as it does any other standard _c_a_p_-_c_o_d_e.  The
           others (iinniitt and lloonnggnnaammee) do not correspond to terminal  capabili‐
           ties.

       •   The  ttppuutt on SVr4-based systems such as Solaris, IRIX64, and HP-UX,
           as well as others such as AIX and Tru64, also support standard _c_a_p_-
           _c_o_d_e operands.

       •   A few platforms such as FreeBSD recognize _t_e_r_m_c_a_p codes rather than
           _t_e_r_m_i_n_f_o capability codes in their respective ttppuutt commands.  Since
           2010,  NetBSD's  ttppuutt  uses  _t_e_r_m_i_n_f_o codes.  Before that, it (like
           FreeBSD) recognized _t_e_r_m_c_a_p codes.

           Beginning in 2021, FreeBSD uses _n_c_u_r_s_e_s ttppuutt, configured  for  both
           _t_e_r_m_i_n_f_o (tested first) and _t_e_r_m_c_a_p (as a fallback).

       Because (apparently) all _c_e_r_t_i_f_i_e_d Unix systems support the full set of
       capability codes, the reason for documenting only a few may not be  ap‐
       parent.

       •   X/Open Curses Issue 7 documents ttppuutt differently, with _c_a_p_-_c_o_d_e and
           the other features used in this implementation.

       •   That is, there are two standards for ttppuutt:  POSIX  (a  subset)  and
           X/Open  Curses (the full implementation).  POSIX documents a subset
           to avoid the complication of including X/Open Curses and the termi‐
           nal capability database.

       •   While  it is certainly possible to write a ttppuutt program without us‐
           ing _c_u_r_s_e_s, no system with a _c_u_r_s_e_s implementation provides a  ttppuutt
           utility that does not also support standard _c_a_p_-_c_o_d_e_s.

       X/Open  Curses  Issue  7 (2009) is the first version to document utili‐
       ties.  However that part of X/Open  Curses  does  not  follow  existing
       practice (that is, System V _c_u_r_s_e_s behavior).

       •   It  assigns  exit status 4 to “invalid operand”, which may have the
           same meaning as “unknown capability”.   For  instance,  the  source
           code for Solaris _x_c_u_r_s_e_s uses the term “invalid” in this case.

       •   It assigns exit status 255 to a numeric variable that is not speci‐
           fied in the _t_e_r_m_i_n_f_o database.  That likely is a documentation  er‐
           ror,  mistaking the “-1” written to the standard output to indicate
           an absent or cancelled numeric capability for  an  (unsigned)  exit
           status.

       The various System V implementations (AIX, HP-UX, Solaris) use the same
       exit statuses as _n_c_u_r_s_e_s.

       NetBSD _c_u_r_s_e_s  documents  exit  statuses  that  correspond  to  neither
       _n_c_u_r_s_e_s nor X/Open Curses.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       Bill  Joy  wrote  a  ttppuutt command during development of 4BSD in October
       1980.  This initial version only cleared the screen, and did  not  ship
       with official distributions.

       System V developed a different ttppuutt command.

       •   SVr2  (1984) provided a rudimentary ttppuutt that checked the parameter
           against each predefined capability and returned  the  corresponding
           value.   This  version of ttppuutt did not use ttppaarrmm(3X) for parameter‐
           ized capabilities.

       •   SVr3 (1987) replaced that with a more extensive program whose  sup‐
           port  for  iinniitt and rreesseett operands (more than half the program) in‐
           corporated the rreesseett feature of BSD ttsseett written by Eric Allman.

       •   SVr4 (1989) added color initialization  by  using  the  oorriigg__ccoolloorrss
           (oocc) and oorriigg__ppaaiirr (oopp) capabilities in its iinniitt logic.

       Keith  Bostic  refactored BSD ttppuutt for shipment in 4.3BSD-Tahoe (1988),
       then replaced it the next year with a new implementation based on  Sys‐
       tem V  ttppuutt.  Bostic's version similarly accepted some parameters named
       for _t_e_r_m_i_n_f_o (pseudo-)capabilities: cclleeaarr, iinniitt, lloonnggnnaammee,  and  rreesseett.
       However,  because  he  had  only _t_e_r_m_c_a_p available, it accepted _t_e_r_m_c_a_p
       codes for other capabilities.  Also, Bostic's BSD ttppuutt did  not  modify
       the terminal modes as the earlier BSD ttsseett had done.

       At  the  same time, Bostic added a shell script named “clear” that used
       ttppuutt to clear the screen.  Both of these appeared in  4.4BSD,  becoming
       the “modern” BSD implementation of ttppuutt.

       The  origin of _n_c_u_r_s_e_s ttppuutt lies outside both System V and BSD, in Ross
       Ridge's _m_y_t_i_n_f_o package, published  on  _c_o_m_p_._s_o_u_r_c_e_s_._u_n_i_x  in  December
       1992.   Ridge's program made more sophisticated use of the terminal ca‐
       pabilities than the BSD program.  Eric Raymond used that  ttppuutt  program
       (and  other  parts  of _m_y_t_i_n_f_o) in _n_c_u_r_s_e_s in June 1995.  Incorporating
       the portions dealing with terminal capabilities almost without  change,
       Raymond  made improvements to the way command-line parameters were han‐
       dled.

       Before _n_c_u_r_s_e_s 6.1 (2018), its ttsseett and ttppuutt utilities differed.

       •   ttsseett was more effective, resetting the terminal modes  and  special
           characters.

       •   On  the  other hand, ttsseett's repertoire of terminal capabilities for
           resetting the terminal was more limited; it had only equivalents of
           rreesseett__11ssttrriinngg  (rrss11), rreesseett__22ssttrriinngg (rrss22), and rreesseett__ffiillee (rrff), and
           not the tab stop and margin update features of ttppuutt.

       The rreesseett program is traditionally an alias for ttsseett due to its ability
       to reset terminal modes and special characters.

       As  of  _n_c_u_r_s_e_s  6.1,  the  “reset”  features  of  the two programs are
       (mostly) the same.  Two minor differences remain.

       •   The ttsseett program waits one second when resetting, in case the  ter‐
           minal happens to be a hardware device.

       •   The  two programs write the terminal initialization strings to dif‐
           ferent streams; that is, standard error for ttsseett and standard  out‐
           put for ttppuutt.

